import"./chunk-2NFLSA4Y.js";var o={electricMotor:{theory:{concept:"[[An electric motor is a device that converts electrical energy into mechanical energy.]] It works because a magnetic field exerts a force on a current-carrying conductor. While it turns, the motor also works as a generator: its spinning coil induces a back EMF that opposes the supply voltage and limits the current."},section:{force:{title:"A current in a magnetic field feels a force",text:"[[A wire carrying a current I across a magnetic field B is pushed sideways with the force F = B\xB7I\xB7\u2113 (\u2113: length of the wire in the field; with N turns the force is N times larger).]] The force is at right angles both to the wire and to the field. In the picture the field points from N to S; a current coming out of the screen (\u2022) is pushed up, a current going into it (\xD7) is pushed down."},torque:{title:"Two forces make the coil turn",text:"The two sides of the coil carry the current in opposite directions, so one is pushed up and the other down. Together they form a turning effect \u2014 a torque. It is largest when the coil lies along the field (\u03B8 = 90\xB0), because then the forces act with the longest lever. When the coil stands at right angles to the field (\u03B8 = 0\xB0 or 180\xB0), the forces pull straight outwards and the torque is zero."},commutator:{title:"The commutator keeps it turning",text:"Without help, the coil would turn only until it stands across the field and then swing back \u2014 try switching the commutator off. The commutator is a ring split into two halves that turn with the coil, with fixed brushes touching them. Just as the coil passes the upright position, each half moves to the other brush, so the current in the coil reverses. The forces flip too, and the coil keeps turning the same way."},deadSpot:{title:"The dead spot",text:"If a single-coil motor stops with its coil exactly across the field, it cannot start again: the torque is zero there. Set the starting angle to 0\xB0 and see. Real motors have many coils at different angles (and often more magnets), so some coil always gives a push and the torque is smooth."},backEmf:{title:"A spinning motor is also a generator",text:"[[A coil turning in a magnetic field produces a voltage by induction \u2014 the back EMF \u03B5.]] It opposes the battery, so the current I = (U \u2212 \u03B5)/R falls as the motor speeds up. That is why a motor draws a large current when it starts or is blocked, and a small one when it runs freely. Surprisingly, a weaker magnet makes an unloaded motor faster: it needs a higher speed to make the back EMF that balances the battery \u2014 but it can push less."},energy:{title:"Where the energy goes",text:"The battery delivers the power U\xB7I. Part of it heats the coil (I\xB2\xB7R), the rest becomes mechanical power \u03C4\xB7\u03C9 that turns the load. A single coil wastes a lot: near the dead spot the current is large but gives little torque, so this motor is at best about 40 % efficient. Large modern motors with many coils reach more than 95 %."},history:{title:"From Faraday to electric cars",text:"In 1821 Michael Faraday made a wire rotate around a magnet \u2014 the first electromagnetic motion. \xC1nyos Jedlik built a small motor with a commutator around 1828, William Sturgeon in 1832, in 1834 Moritz Jacobi built a practical motor, and in 1838 his motors drove a boat on the Neva river. Today motors run fans, washing machines, drones, trains and electric cars; many modern motors are 'brushless': electronics switch the current instead of a commutator."},check:{title:"Check your understanding",text:"In which position is the torque on the coil largest? (When the coil lies along the field, \u03B8 = 90\xB0.) What does the commutator do? (It reverses the current in the coil every half turn.) What happens if you swap the battery? (The motor turns the other way.) Why does a blocked motor get hot? (Without back EMF the current is large, and all the power heats the coil.) Try each one in the simulation."}},formula:{force:"Force on one side of the coil (\u2113: its length in the field)",torque:"Torque on the coil (\u03B8: angle between the coil's normal and the field)",backEmf:"Back EMF induced in the turning coil",current:"Current: the back EMF works against the battery",power:"Power balance: battery = heat + mechanical work",steady:"Final speed of a heavy rotor (b: bearing friction)"},variable:{side:"Length of a coil side in the field (4 cm)",area:"Area of the coil (4 cm \xD7 3 cm)",omega:"Angular speed"},assumption:{coil:"One rectangular coil in a uniform field; the coil's inductance is ignored, so the current follows the voltage at once.",commutator:"An ideal commutator: it switches exactly at 0\xB0 and 180\xB0, without sparks or contact resistance.",friction:"The bearings have a small viscous friction and a little static friction; the load torque always opposes the motion.",slow:"Motion is shown 20 times slower than reality; readings retain physical speed, time and current. The coil, magnets, shaft and commutator are schematic 3D objects, not to scale. The drawn winding represents the selected turn count. Current and force arrows show direction; their sizes are illustrative.",steady:"The final speed, efficiency and output power are calculated for a heavy rotor (speed nearly constant during a turn)."},param:{voltage:"Battery voltage",field:"Strength of the magnets (field B)",turns:"Number of turns in the coil",load:"Load on the shaft (torque)",commutator:"Commutator (split ring)",startAngle:"Starting angle of the coil",resistance:"Coil resistance",timeStep:"Time step"},hint:{voltage:"A higher voltage drives more current. A negative voltage means the battery is turned around.",field:"A stronger magnet pushes harder on the current.",turns:"Each turn of wire adds to the force \u2014 and to the back EMF.",load:"How hard the motor has to work, e.g. lifting a weight or turning a fan.",commutator:"Switch it off to see why every DC motor needs one.",startAngle:"The angle between the coil's normal and the field when the motor is switched on. At 0\xB0 or 180\xB0 the coil stands across the field.",resistance:"Electrical resistance of the coil wire: it turns some of the energy into heat."},preset:{reversed:"\u22126 V (reversed)",v3:"3 V",v6:"6 V",v12:"12 V",deadSpot:"0\xB0 (dead spot)",a30:"30\xB0",a90:"90\xB0"},measure:{rpm:"Speed now",steadyRpm:"Final speed (calculated)",current:"Current",torque:"Torque of the coil now",backEmf:"Back EMF",startTorque:"Starting torque",angle:"Angle of the coil",powerIn:"Power from the battery",powerHeat:"Heat in the coil",powerMechanical:"Mechanical power",efficiency:"Efficiency (final, with load)",output:"Useful output power (final)",motorConstant:"Motor constant"},graph:{speed:"Speed vs time",currentEmf:"Current (A) and back EMF (V) vs time"},view:{force3d:"Force on each long side: {value}",north:"N",south:"S",field:"field B = {value} (N \u2192 S)",commutator:"commutator and brushes \xB7 {voltage}",slipRings:"no commutator \xB7 {voltage}",status:"{rpm} \xB7 I = {current}",rocks:"No commutator: the coil only rocks",deadSpot:"Dead spot: no torque",stalled:"Stalled by the load",legend:"Arrows along the wire: conventional current \xB7 transverse arrows: force \xB7 schematic 3D rig",forcesOption:"Forces on the coil",fieldOption:"Magnetic field",commutatorOption:"Commutator"},challenge:{reverse:{title:"The other way round",description:"Make the motor spin in the opposite direction (at least 1000 rpm).",hint:"What happens to the forces if the current flows the other way?"},faster:{title:"Faster!",description:"Make the unloaded motor reach a final speed of at least 8000 rpm.",hint:"More voltage helps. And try a weaker magnet \u2014 the result may surprise you."},strongStart:{title:"A strong start",description:"Give the motor a starting torque of at least 0.04 N\xB7m.",hint:"\u03C4 = N\xB7B\xB7I\xB7A\xB7|sin \u03B8|: more current, a stronger magnet or more turns."},deadSpot:{title:"Find the dead spot",description:"Switch the motor on in a position where it cannot start by itself.",hint:"The torque is zero when the coil stands at right angles to the field."},heavyLoad:{title:"Lift the heavy load",description:"With a load of at least 0.01 N\xB7m, keep the final speed at 3000 rpm or more.",hint:"Under load the motor needs more push: a higher voltage, or more turns and a stronger magnet."},efficient:{title:"Waste less",description:"Reach an efficiency of at least 35 % while giving at least 0.5 W of useful power.",hint:"Heat I\xB2R is the loss. The motor is most efficient when it runs fast with a moderate load."},power:{title:"10 watts",description:"Make the motor deliver at least 10 W of useful mechanical power.",hint:"Output power = load torque \xD7 speed. Too little load wastes the speed, too much stalls the motor."}}}},a={sim:o};export{a as default,o as sim};
